| **Prior week** | 2026-10-17 → 2026-10-23 | $5,377.43 | 28 | $192.05 |

**Week-over-week (from `compare_windows`, last_week vs prior_week):** spend **up** (+$2,651.92, +49.3%), conversions **up** (+14, +50.0%), CPA **flat** (−$0.87, −0.5%). The same figures appear in `/weekly-report-2026-10-24_to_2026-10-30.md`. Cross-check: the two weeks sum to $13,406.78 vs $25,421.90 for 30 days — the remainder is Oct 1–16, consistent with the 30-day window covering more than the comparison fortnight.

**Likely driver of the +49.3% spend:** per the campaign-goals wiki decisions log, ~15% of search budget was deliberately shifted to brand awareness in July, and the weekly report attributes the added delivery to that decision. Conversions scaled with spend, so CPA was unaffected. Treat this as the documented hypothesis, not a measured per-campaign fact (see below).

## Per-campaign breakdown — **not available, stated explicitly**

My Google Ads tools return **account-level totals only**. There is no per-campaign breakdown for spend, conversions, or CPA for either "Search - product terms" or the brand awareness video campaign — I verified the workspace (report, wiki, proposals, mutations log) and none contains campaign-level metrics; the only campaign figures anywhere are the account totals quoted above.

**Therefore I cannot confirm which campaign is best-performing.** Additional limitations specific to your question:

- The two known campaigns have **different goals** (qualified leads vs impressions at target CPA), so even a full spend/conversions/CPA split wouldn't rank them — and platform conversions ≠ qualified leads. Judging "Search - product terms" on its actual goal requires **warehouse** pipeline data (leads/opportunities), which I don't have access to and which must not be joined to these platform figures.
- No impressions data was returned, so the video campaign's goal metric can't be evaluated either.
